ding a man than  on their studies she attends a speed  dating course reciting judy garland  underneath this prim exterior there are  depths of emotion  romantic longings this is a line from  the 1948 film the pirate and points at  jia's longing for love something her  mother says she can't feel because she's  not  human jia ends up bringing a man home  from the bar and as they make love  hairy tentacles emerge from her orifices  horrify  allowing her to gain all the memories of  that person's life  for example this poor victim we see him  get married have a baby and climb a  mountain that is of course before he  dies in a giant explosion of blood and  guts  as we'll find out jia is a kumiho a  spirit of a thousand-year-old fox a  creature from korean folklore that is  manifested in the form of a beautiful  woman to seduce boys and steal their  souls  years ago jia's mother made a deal with  a mudang a korean shaman  the spirit of kumiho was instilled in  jia's body to kill father a man who  molested jia as a child  while mother watched and did nothing she  was a woman with a child out of wedlock  and this man was her only way to be  rescued from  shame and disgrace it was the price she  had to pay  so now jia's mother encourages her  daughter to kill in the hopes that once  she collects a hundred souls her real  daughter will be returned to her  back at the cinema there's a brief  cartoon stating that anyone who pits one  person against another  is trying to rob you of your freedoms a  fitting cartoon since moments later  marks the start of the korean war  a war that pits two sides against one  another it's no wonder then that after  we see this cartoon we hear an american  soldier saying he's fighting for freedom  america is here to fight for your  freedom gia's mother is concerned that  she hasn't brought home anyone in a  month she just needs a few more souls  before all the memories she's absorbed  will be wiped clean that's because  gia is struggling with whether or not  she wants to change at all  her friend youngja tells her it's okay  to be different young herself is a  communist something that will get you  strung up  this pep talk would be great if it  weren't for the fact that gia isn't  human at all after one of the nurses is  found out to be a communist spy they're  taken away to be shot for many of you  this may have been a jarring scene as  it's not often we're shown  america commits such heinous war crimes  but if you want to learn more i suggest  you check out the pbs documentary kill  em all  america war crimes in korea it's here we  meet atticus who not only shoots an  innocent woman  but will later find through gs powers  engaged in acts of torture  several months later jia's only form of  escape the theater is closed with red  graffiti saying  red owned communist sympathizer she'll  break down when she finds that atticus  the man who killed her best friend  is a patient at her hospital later  vowing to kill him atticus asked her to  read the last few chapters from the  count of monte cristo his father's  favorite book we'll later learn one of  the reasons montrose loves this book is  that it's about a man who gets revenge  after facing oppression  and that alexandro dumas the novel's  author was a black man she refuses to  read it to atticus instead  spoiling the entire ending for him what  she doesn't know is that atticus has  already read it and that the ending she  gave out is that of the 1934 film of the  same name  where the two leads rekindle their  relationships whereas in the book  they don't atticus remarks i see what i  thought that change would make for a  better movie  just like the book didn't see the two  lovers end up together this same can be  said for atticus and gia  there's a great scene where jia asks  atticus and a korean-american soldier  why they fight for a country that  doesn't treat them as equals for the  korean american  other than the fact that he was drafted  he feels like he 